                    <physDesc>
                        <objectDesc>
                            <supportDesc>
                                <support><p>Described by Gentili as surviving in two large fragments (but the second is nowhere described, unless the photographed fragment is the two recomposed), damaged on the sides and below.</p>
                                    <material ana="#material.inorganic.stone.sedimentary_rock.limestone" type="stone.limestone" subtype="unverified" ref="http://www.eagle-network.eu/voc/material/lod/66.html">limestone</material>
                                    <objectType ana="#object.block" ref="http://www.eagle-network.eu/voc/objtyp/lod/189.html">block</objectType>
                                    <dimensions><!--Gentili-->
                                        <height unit="cm">25</height>
                                        <width unit="cm">38</width>
                                        <depth unit="cm">27</depth>
                                    </dimensions>
                                </support>
                                <condition ana="#condition.fragment"/>
		    </supportDesc>
                            <layoutDesc>
                                <layout><p>Four lines of Greek are preserved across the upper front face, seemingly centred on the stone with varying vacats at the line ends. It is impossible to know how many more lines might be lost below.</p>
                                    <rs ana="#execution.chiselled" ref="http://www.eagle-network.eu/voc/writing/lod/1">chiselled</rs>
                                	<damage ana="#text_condition.incomplete"/>
			</layout>
                            </layoutDesc>
                        </objectDesc>
                        <handDesc>
                            <handNote><p>Broad regular letters of high Hellenistic form with minimal terminal serifs. Alpha has straight bar; Epsilon a slighly shorter middle bar; Kappa has nearly full length arms (but slightly variable); Mu has vertical outer strokes, half length inner strokes; omicron is almost full size; Sigma has parallel outer strokes; Phi has slightly extended vertical; Omega is full size, slightly ovoid, and almost closed.</p>
                                <locus from="line1" to="line4">Line 1-4</locus>
                                <dimensions type="letterHeight">
                                    <height unit="mm" atLeast="20" atMost="20">20</height><!--Gentili-->
                                </dimensions>
                                <locus from="line1" to="line2">Interlineation line 1 to 2</locus>
                                <dimensions type="interlinear">
                                    <height unit="mm"/>
                                </dimensions>
                            </handNote>
                        </handDesc>
